Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

/*****************************************************************************
    spellScratch -- emitLeaf's OWN fixture, and it exists because emitLeaf is
    about to be rewritten in kant and there was nothing to diff the rewrite
    against.

    THE ORACLE IS THE FUNCTION BEING REPLACED. Captured while the C++ emitLeaf
    is still the only implementation, `genLadder/spell.target` is a byte-exact
    statement of what every plan kind spells under every sink. Same discipline
    as rung4.target holding emitted text against the compiled-in method.

    WHY THE LADDER TARGETS ARE NOT ENOUGH ON THEIR OWN -- they do gate emitLeaf,
    because it writes every term spelling inside them, but only for the kinds the
    ladder reaches. Nothing in the ladder is a LABELLED literal (every Scaf term
    is noLabel), so LITTO is reached by no rung and BOTH of its spellings, litTo
    and litOption, were UNGATED. `CodE` plans as a SEQ of two LITTO terms, so the
    real grammar covers the kind the scaffolds cannot.

    THE ROWS, and each one is here for a named kind rather than for coverage's
    own sake:
        Scaf     LIT                     the plain literal
        Scaf2    LIT LIT                 two of them, indices baked 1 and 2
        ScafB    CALL LIT                a reference term
        ScafC    MANY LIT                the repetition helper's call site
        ScafE    LIT OPT(CALL) LIT       OPT wrapping a reference -- emitLeaf's
                                         one SELF-RECURSIVE branch
        ScafF    LIT OPT(LIT) LIT        OPT wrapping a literal
        CodE     LITTO LITTO             litTo and litOption, the ungated pair
        ScafALT  CALL CALL               an ALT fold, whose sink is `into`
        Limit    (refusal)               a REFUSED rule -- the loud-refusal path
                                         is behaviour too, and it is the one a
                                         rewrite is most likely to quietly drop

    Both sinks are printed for EVERY node even where the fold could never ask
    for one. LITTO is the only kind whose text differs between them; printing
    both everywhere costs two lines and means the target moves if that ever
    stops being true.

    ⚠ ONE HOLE, NAMED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: in `litTo(t1,label,"{","{")` the
    literal and the slot are the SAME STRING, so this target cannot tell those
    two positions apart -- a rewrite that swapped them would pass. That is not
    an accident of the fixture, it is a property of planTerm: a literal term's
    slot IS its text. Every LITTO in the whole census is like this (`CodE`'s two,
    and JSONtoken's `false`/`true`). So there is no rule to add that would close
    it; closing it needs a labelled literal whose tag differs from its text, and
    the grammar has none. Recorded so nobody reads this row as stronger than it is.

    RUN:   <binary> incant/spellScratch
    POP:   part of `sh genLadder/pop.sh`
*****************************************************************************/
registry(cOMMANDs);
define dumpSpellings immediateAction=dumpSpellings; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define spellMode immediateAction=spellMode; ;

/*  The kant emitter. A stub until it holds a `spellLeaf`, at which point
    emitLeaf's fork routes here instead of running its own body -- and
    spellMode's line below is what says which of the two actually ran, since
    both produce the same target and a silent fork cannot be told apart.

    THE fILEs ENTRY IS NOT OPTIONAL. `include(name)` resolves through the fILEs
    registry, not through the filesystem: an unregistered name is passed to
    getFile verbatim and looked up relative to the CURRENT DIRECTORY, which
    fails with "could not open file: genEmit" and exits 2. Registered here rather
    than in incant/setup deliberately -- this is the fixture's dependency, not
    the language's.  */
registry(fILEs);
define genEmit File='incant/genEmit'; ;
include(genEmit);

register(Ladder);
define
    Scaf  isRule "x"-;
    Scaf2 isRule "{"- "}"-;
    ScafA isRule "a"-;
    ScafB isRule ScafA "b"-;
    ScafC isRule ScafA+ "c"-;
    ScafE isRule "e"- ScafA? "f"-;
    ScafF isRule "f"- ","?- "g"-;
    ScafI isRule "i"-;
    ScafALT isRule
        ScafA;
        ScafI;
    ;
search reset stack Grokking Spellers Ladder UnitTests Utilities list;

spellMode('');
dumpSpellings('Scaf');
dumpSpellings('Scaf2');
dumpSpellings('ScafB');
dumpSpellings('ScafC');
dumpSpellings('ScafE');
dumpSpellings('ScafF');
dumpSpellings('ScafALT');
dumpSpellings('CodE');
dumpSpellings('Limit');
stop();
